pipewire-rtp-zeroconf (0.1.5-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Rename project, source package, binary packages and drop-in confs
    from pipewire-net-zeroconf* to pipewire-rtp-zeroconf*. New name
    matches the GitHub repo and is more descriptive (RTP/mDNS transport,
    not "any network").
    Drop-in confs renamed:
      30-net-zeroconf-publish.conf  → 30-rtp-zeroconf-publish.conf
      31-net-zeroconf-discover.conf → 31-rtp-zeroconf-discover.conf
    Metadata key prefix changed from "pipewire-net-zeroconf.*" to
    "pipewire-rtp-zeroconf.*" — stored per-card profile choices and
    enable/disable toggles from older installs will NOT carry over;
    re-pick once after upgrading.
    Binary package names changed:
      pipewire-net-zeroconf{-publish,-discover}  →
      pipewire-rtp-zeroconf{-publish,-discover}
    Old packages are not transitionally replaced; uninstall the
    pipewire-net-zeroconf* packages by hand before installing the new
    ones to avoid both sides loading at once.

 -- pipewire-rtp-zeroconf <maintainer@example.invalid>  Sun, 17 May 2026 14:00:00 +0000

pipewire-rtp-zeroconf (0.1.4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * discover: card_publish_request now embeds an FNV hash of
    (peer_host, card_name) into the Avahi service name instead of
    truncating card_name to 20 chars. Two cards on the same speaker host
    whose card_name shares the first 20 chars (the common case —
    alsa_card.pci-0000_XX_YY.Z is 26 chars and only the last 6 differ)
    were collapsing to the same Avahi service name → Local name collision
    → only the first card's back-channel was published → the second
    card's mic never streamed (publish-host had no idea workpc was
    waiting for it). Now both requests get distinct names + distinct
    ports.
  * peer-device: use designated-initializer form for spa_param_info[] to
    silence -Wmissing-field-initializers warnings on the user/padding
    fields.

 -- pipewire-rtp-zeroconf <maintainer@example.invalid>  Sun, 17 May 2026 13:50:00 +0000

pipewire-rtp-zeroconf (0.1.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * peer-device: impl_add_listener now forces a full change_mask
    (PROPS|PARAMS) before the first emit_info, mirroring
    alsa-acp-device.c's full=true path. Without this a peer_device that
    was created from an Avahi service and never set_param'd (e.g. an
    Output-only HDMI/DP card with no user click) shipped to clients with
    params:(0) — EnumProfile/Profile never reached pavucontrol, the
    card showed up at the bottom of the config tab with an empty
    profile combo. Triggered by HDMI subdevice transitions (acp swaps
    hdmi-stereo → hdmi-stereo-extra1 reusing the PW node id; our
    discover side correctly destroys+recreates the peer_card and
    peer_device, but the new device's first listener wasn't getting
    PARAMS bit so its profile list never showed up).

 -- pipewire-rtp-zeroconf <maintainer@example.invalid>  Sun, 17 May 2026 13:40:00 +0000

pipewire-rtp-zeroconf (0.1.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * peer-device: persistent spa_device_info + spa_param_info[] in struct,
    XOR-toggle SPA_PARAM_INFO_SERIAL flag on emit_info (mirrors
    alsa-acp-device.c). Without this pw_impl_device never invalidated its
    Profile param cache on PW 1.0.x, so pavucontrol/pulse-bridge saw a
    stale Profile.current forever after the first read and the user's
    choice "flipped back" each session.
  * peer-device: sync device.profile property from both impl_set_param
    and peer_device_set_available_dirs (was only set from the internal
    set_active_dirs path), keeping property consistent with Profile.current
    for WP find-best-profile and pulse-bridge card-active calculation.
  * discover: dedup duplicate Avahi service announcements (IPv4 + IPv6
    on the same interface produced two peer_devices for one card, every
    set_param processed twice). Added peer_card.loaded_dirs bitmask set
    before tunnel attachment so the second event for the same
    (host, card, direction) is dropped early with a "dup service" info log.
  * discover: when a card's second direction service arrives, re-apply
    the stored profile / default after growing available_dirs — previously
    active_dirs stayed at whatever single-direction default the card was
    first created with.
  * peer-device: emit SPA_PARAM_PROFILE_classes Struct alongside each
    Profile (Audio/Sink/Source per direction) so WP policy understands
    which node classes a profile activates.
  * docs/2026-05-17-profile-cache-fix.md: full debug history.

 -- pipewire-rtp-zeroconf <maintainer@example.invalid>  Sun, 17 May 2026 13:00:00 +0000

pipewire-rtp-zeroconf (0.1.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Split into three binary packages:
      - pipewire-rtp-zeroconf-publish   (speaker-host)
      - pipewire-rtp-zeroconf-discover  (app-host)
      - pipewire-rtp-zeroconf           (metapackage)
    Most hosts only need one of the two; the previous single-package
    layout caused both sides to load by default which led to spurious
    rtp-sink connect errors on speaker-only hosts.
  * Drop-in confs renamed and now ship one per module:
      30-rtp-zeroconf-publish.conf, 31-rtp-zeroconf-discover.conf.
  * Suppress upstream rtp-source's bogus IGMP-recovery noise in unicast
    bindings via igmp.check.interval.sec = 31536000.
  * Avahi name collision: retry add_service up to 16 times with
    avahi_alternative_service_name() to handle multiple cards sharing a
    description.

 -- pipewire-rtp-zeroconf <maintainer@example.invalid>  Sat, 16 May 2026 19:00:00 +0000

pipewire-rtp-zeroconf (0.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release.
    - libpipewire-module-mdns-rtp-publish: per-card Avahi entries with
      libpipewire-module-rtp-source as receiver.
    - libpipewire-module-mdns-rtp-discover: per-peer-card virtual sink/source
      via libpipewire-module-rtp-sink/source, profile selection through
      a SPA Device with Off/Output/Input/Output+Input profiles.

